2026-09-18 | offline guarantee audit: src/the_oracle/offline.py (new) + cli.py + real_engine_smoke.py + scripts/doctor.py + scripts/manage_install.py | Defect: the offline marker (`.oracle_offline`) was honoured only by the generated shell launchers and by `manage_install run_gui()`. Every model-loading entry point that does not go through those — the console script `the-oracle render` / `the-oracle gui` invoked directly, `scripts/doctor.py` (and therefore `manage_install doctor`, `./doctor_oracle_tts.sh`, `./oracle doctor`), and `scripts/real_engine_smoke.py` — loaded models with offline resolution off, so on an air-gapped install they could reach out instead of resolving from the seeded cache. Proven by tests written first and failing per entry point: `pipeline saw [None, None]`, `launch_gui saw [None]`, `probe environment carried [None]`, `smoke saw [(None, False)]`. Root cause of the mechanism, measured on this machine with a seeded cache and an unreachable endpoint, resolving a model the cache cannot satisfy: HF_HUB_OFFLINE set BEFORE huggingface_hub's import → 0.0s; set AFTER → 23.0s across five retries with an 8s backoff; set after PLUS forcing the import-time constant → 0.0s. `huggingface_hub` captures the variable during its own import, so a late `os.environ` write is silently ineffective — and the CLI/doctor/smoke all import the engine (hence huggingface_hub) before their entry point runs. | Fix: new `src/the_oracle/offline.py` is the single owner — the marker name (`OFFLINE_MARKER_FILENAME`), the variable set (`OFFLINE_ENV` = HF_HUB_OFFLINE + TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE), `is_offline_install()`, and `apply_offline_environment()` which exports the variables AND, when `huggingface_hub.constants` is already imported, forces its `HF_HUB_OFFLINE` (looked up in `sys.modules`, not through the package: the package attribute is lazy and raises, which silently skipped the patch in my first attempt). Wired into `cli.main` (covers render and gui), `doctor.main` (before `run()`, so the subprocess probes inherit it through `_probe_environment`), `real_engine_smoke.main`, and `manage_install.run_gui`, which now consumes the owner instead of its own inline marker check. `manage_install.OFFLINE_MARKER_FILENAME` is imported from the owner too, so the launcher generation, the installer, and the runtime check cannot drift apart. | Verified: tests/test_offline_guarantee.py, 10 tests — one per entry point (console render, console gui, doctor, real-engine smoke, installer run action), the owner's scoping/idempotence, a no-marker control, seeded-cache resolution for all three loader families in a fresh process (marker + seeded cache + closed endpoint, with `socket.connect` forbidden → chatterbox, turbo and the transformers helper all resolve inside the cache), and a fail-fast test for an unsatisfiable model. Proven load-bearing by mutation, each reverted: removing the call from cli.main fails 2 tests, from real_engine_smoke 1, from doctor 1, and neutering the constant force fails 5. Real end-to-end on this machine's seeded cache: with the marker present, `HF_HUB_OFFLINE` unset in the parent and `HF_ENDPOINT` pointed at a closed port, `scripts/real_engine_smoke.py` rendered successfully (23.4s, FLAC produced); `scripts/doctor.py --ci` with full model init reported `overall_ready=True, chatterbox_init=True` in 7.5s with the marker and gave a byte-identical verdict without it (timings excluded) — so enforcement changes how resolution happens, not what the doctor concludes. Doctor idempotent: two consecutive wrapper runs byte-identical, exit 0. Suites: 916 passed / 0 failed (this tree); 915 passed / 1 skipped (clean archive copy). Nothing pushed.

2026-09-18 | offline guarantee audit: findings reported, NOT fixed | Three gaps found outside the model-loading entry points, deliberately left alone as adjacent scope: (1) the Vulkan audio.cpp `.gguf` model is NOT in the offline bundle (`scripts/build_offline_bundle.py` has no audio.cpp references) and `the-oracle setup-vulkan` downloads it, so `--inference-backend vulkan` cannot resolve its model from the seeded cache on an offline install — the backend itself only loads a local file or fails, it never fetches. (2) the LanguageTool snapshot (hundreds of MB) is likewise not in the bundle; the grammar path is offline-safe in the sense that `_language_tool_download_ready()` is checked first and the render proceeds via the local fallback, but when the snapshot is absent `_warm_language_tool_download()` starts a background download, which an offline install would attempt. (3) `scripts/download_models.py` and `scripts/build_offline_bundle.py` intentionally do not honour the marker: their job is to fetch (prefetch / build the bundle), so honouring it would defeat them. Also honest about what did NOT break: the pinned-SHA loaders (the engine's `snapshot_download(revision=<pin>)` and the transformers helper models) resolve locally from a properly seeded cache even with offline mode off — measured against this machine's real hub cache — so the guarantee's value is no traffic at all on an air-gapped box, a fast clear failure instead of a 23s retry storm for anything the cache cannot satisfy, and never silently fetching a different revision. Recommended next pass: bundle the audio.cpp model (or make `setup-vulkan` detect the marker and refuse with a clear message), and skip the LanguageTool warm download when the marker is present.

2026-09-18 | .github/workflows/ci.yml + scripts/windows_install_smoke.py (new) + tests/test_windows_install_smoke.py (new) | Gap: the suite pinned what the installer *writes* (tests/test_install_boundary.py asserts the exact `the-oracle.cmd` and Start Menu entry contents) but nothing had ever proven those files *execute*. Recorded commands and file bytes are not proof that a Windows `.cmd` launcher runs, and the Start Menu entry starts a Qt event loop that has to be killed rather than awaited. Content inspection of the wrapper body — the only thing possible on this Linux host — proves nothing about cmd.exe. | Added a second CI job, `windows-install-smoke` (windows-latest, 45-minute timeout, kept out of the push/PR matrix gate because it downloads the pinned checkpoint), and scripts/windows_install_smoke.py, which: builds an isolated environment (USERPROFILE/HOME/APPDATA/LOCALAPPDATA redirected into a throwaway profile, HF_HOME and PIP_CACHE_DIR to explicitly named directories the cache step can key on, the profile's launcher dir prepended to PATH, QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen, and HF_HUB_OFFLINE/TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE *removed* because this job covers the online install and an inherited flag would make the doctor fail for the wrong reason); runs `manage_install.py install --pytorch-runtime cpu`; executes the managed launcher for real (`cmd /d /c the-oracle.cmd --help`, requiring exit 0 and `usage` in the output); then launches the Start Menu entry and waits for the GUI to report `mainwindow_built` in Output/logs/gui_launch_timing.json before killing the process tree with `taskkill /T /F /PID`. The cache key is `hashFiles('src/the_oracle/models/pins.py')`, so a pin bump can never reuse a stale checkpoint. The profile path contains a space on purpose — the hostile case this repo's own checkout path already exercises. | Verified against the real code, not asserted: the `.cmd` template passes `%*` through (`"%VENV_ENTRYPOINT%" %*`, manage_install.py:204) so `--help` reaches the CLI rather than launching the GUI, and the Start Menu entry is `call "<managed>" gui %*` (line 420); `the-oracle --help` really prints `usage: ...`; `install --pytorch-runtime cpu` really is an accepted flag (`choices=["auto","cpu","cuda"]`, line 600); `launch_gui()` writes the timing log unconditionally and to a SOURCE-relative path (`Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/Output/logs`, app_gui.py:6385), which is independent of the working directory and identical to the constant the smoke waits on; the script imports stdlib only, which it must because CI runs it before any venv exists (its own subprocesses use `sys.executable`). New suite tests/test_windows_install_smoke.py, 14 tests on any host: the isolated environment, that the launcher paths come from `manage_install`'s own rules rather than a second copy (a drift check), every GUI-wait path (live process, slow start polled not failed, dead process with exit code, timeout, stale log from a dead process, unusable logs), the refusal to run off Windows (exit 2), and the CI job's declared contract. Assertions proven load-bearing by mutation, all reverted: dropping ONE PowerShell continuation backtick from the step fails the workflow test, because the invocation is reassembled from continuations and the `--hf-home` flag then lands in a separate meaningless command (this mutation originally slipped through and drove the structural rewrite of that assertion — the flag checks were substring-based and matched a truncated command); bumping chatterbox-tts or removing `--no-index` was covered by the earlier install-boundary pass. | Honest boundary, stated plainly: this job has never executed on a Windows runner here. What is proven on this host is the job's declared contract, the script's environment/layout/wait logic, and its refusal path; the first real proof of `.cmd` execution, Start Menu execution and the offscreen GUI window remains CI's first run of this job. Suites: 930 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors (this tree); 929 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed / 0 errors (clean `git archive HEAD` copy, 195 tracked files, no .venv, no build/ — the skip is the Vulkan guard for the absent gitignored audio.cpp build). Nothing pushed.

2026-09-18 | scripts/windows_install_smoke.py + tests/test_windows_install_smoke.py | Defect found while probing the new helper: `make_layout()` permanently wrote the throwaway profile into the shared process environment (`os.environ["APPDATA"] = <profile>/AppData/Roaming`) and never restored it. APPDATA is needed only while `manage_install.managed_launcher_path()` / `start_menu_launcher_path()` resolve the Windows rules, but the write outlived that: the profile is a temp directory deleted when the test ends, so the environment left behind pointed at a path that no longer exists, for every subsequent test in the session. Measured before the fix (no monkeypatch, so the leak was visible directly): `APPDATA before: None` → `APPDATA after : /tmp/tmp7mjqokfc/p/AppData/Roaming` → LEAKED. The leak was also load-bearing for a test, which is how it stayed hidden: test_layout_agrees_with_the_installer_module compared `make_layout`'s result against `manage_install.managed_launcher_path()` resolved AFTER the call, and only agreed because the leaked value was still sitting in the environment. | Root-cause fix: `make_layout()` saves the previous APPDATA, sets the profile's value for the duration of the two path resolutions, and restores it in a `finally` (unset stays unset). Verified live: `env identical before/after: True`, APPDATA back to None, the returned paths unchanged, and the non-Windows refusal still exits 2. The drift test now resolves the installer's expectation under the same APPDATA the smoke passes in, via monkeypatch, instead of inheriting whatever the helper happened to leave behind. Regression test test_layout_does_not_leak_the_temp_profile_into_the_environment covers both cases (a pre-existing value is preserved; an absent variable stays absent) and FAILED against the unfixed code with `assert '/tmp/pytest-.../AppData/Roaming' == 'C:\\existing\\Roaming'`. Proven load-bearing by mutation: reinstating the leak (restore removed) fails exactly that test; the restore was re-applied and the file's diff against the fixed version verified identical. | Suites: 930 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors (this tree); 929 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed / 0 errors (pristine). Nothing pushed.

2026-09-18 | .github/workflows/ci.yml + scripts/doctor_idempotence.py (new) + tests/test_doctor_idempotence.py (new) | Gap: the gate had regressed twice in the same class — a check grading state it had created on an earlier run (the real-engine readiness PASS citing a smoke FLAC it never produced; the voice-source fallback count inflated by reference clips the previous run wrote) — and neither instance was visible in a single run. Only the suite's in-process, heavily stubbed test compared two consecutive runs, so nothing in CI would have noticed either regression. Root cause of the gap: the comparison existed as an assertion inside a test, not as a gate on the artefact CI actually runs. | Added scripts/doctor_idempotence.py, which runs doctor.py twice in one mode, captures both `--json` reports, and fails if they differ, naming every differing field with both values plus a unified diff of the two reports with the registered measurements masked. Only the paths in MEASURED_FIELDS may differ: `chatterbox_init.seconds` (the measurement the human report interpolates as `in 8.392s`) and `deterministic_smoke.runtime_seconds`. The registry is deliberately narrow and self-maintaining: an exemption applies only where BOTH sides are numbers, so registering a container path cannot excuse everything beneath it; a registered path absent from BOTH reports is itself a failure (the field was renamed, so the exemption now protects nothing); the tuple is pinned by a test so widening it is a reviewable diff; and any unregistered field that differs fails loudly with an instruction to either register it or fix the check. Wired into the CI test matrix as one step with no `if:` and no `shell:` override — a single command, so each runner's default shell (bash, pwsh) runs it identically and the check covers Linux and Windows rather than silently one of them — using the same `--skip-model-init --ci` mode as the doctor steps already in that job, so it measures the gate as it is actually invoked. | Evidence: with those flags exactly one field varies between two runs on this tree — `deterministic_smoke.runtime_seconds` 0.909 vs 0.893 — and nothing else in the whole report, which is what makes a text-level comparison of the full report the right check rather than a heuristics-based one; each run costs ~10s, so the CI step is ~20s. In full mode (no `--skip-model-init`) `chatterbox_init.seconds` is a real measurement (8.392 observed) and varies, so the second registry entry is load-bearing rather than speculative. Proven load-bearing end to end by re-introducing the historical defect: with `ensure_real_engine_inputs(output_root)` put back into `_real_engine_smoke_status` (the exact pre-fix code) and `build/real_engine_smoke/inputs` parked so the mutation starts where the bug appeared, the checker exits 1 reporting `voice_sources.fallback_clip_count: 0 != 2`; doctor.py was then restored and its diff against HEAD verified empty, the mutation-created inputs deleted, and the parked inputs put back so the tree is exactly as found. Five mutations of the new script, each reverted: comparison disabled -> 9 tests fail; the exemption widened to a container path -> 3 fail; the registry drift guard removed -> the rename test fails; the two runs given different flags -> the same-mode test fails; a real state field (`voice_sources.fallback_clip_count`) registered as a measurement -> 7 fail. One defect was found in the script itself this way: `differences(measured=...)` was only half-wired (the exemption read the module global while only the drift guard used the argument), so the narrowness of an exemption could not be tested at all; threading `measured` through `_compare` fixed it and the subtree case is now pinned by tests. | Honest boundary, stated plainly: the check asserts only that two runs AGREE, and deliberately does not fail because the doctor reports the install is not ready — that verdict is the doctor's own exit code and the sibling CI steps already gate on it, so the new step cannot be used as a second readiness gate and does not turn a genuinely broken machine green. On THIS machine a full-mode run has both invocations exiting 1 (the known out-of-repo stale `~/.local/bin/the-oracle` launcher, plus turbo), and the checker still exits 0 because the two reports are identical. Suites: 949 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors (this tree); 948 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed / 0 errors (clean archive copy, 197 tracked files, no .venv, no build/). Nothing pushed.

2026-09-18 | tests/test_doctor_read_only.py (new) + tests/helpers.py | Gap: the two regressions in this class (reference clips written into build/real_engine_smoke/inputs and then counted by the voice-source audit; a readiness PASS citing a smoke FLAC that was never produced) were being caught AFTER the fact, by comparing two reports. Nothing asserted the underlying property -- that a check reads and never writes -- so a third check could reintroduce it and only be noticed if its write happened to change a report the idempotence check compares, and any check whose write is invisible there (a cache, a log, a downloaded asset, a file another component reads later) would not be noticed at all. | Added a whole-tree content snapshot taken either side of a real doctor run, as a reusable helper (tests/helpers.py: repo_tree_snapshot + snapshot_differences returning created/changed/removed) and a guard test that runs the real doctors script in the mode CI gates on and fails if anything outside the one directory the doctor declares was created, changed or removed. The allowlist is not assumed: a measured run showed the deterministic smoke rewrites exactly three files in build/doctor_deterministic_smoke/render_project_txt/ (render_plan.json, render_plan.json.bak, logs/render_timings.json -- the project it renders IS that check's subject) and creates nothing anywhere, so build/doctor_deterministic_smoke/ is the single declared output and every other path is read-only. Cost was measured rather than guessed: the tree holds 7022 files and 3.4 GB, 3.35 GB of it the git-ignored native build clone (audio.cpp/), so files <=1 MiB are hashed byte-for-byte while larger ones are fingerprinted by size+mtime -- a rewrite changes one or the other, so detection is unaffected, and two snapshots cost ~1.5s instead of ~15s. Git-ignored paths are deliberately INCLUDED: the regressions lived in git-ignored space, so honouring .gitignore here would have hidden exactly the writes being looked for. | The decisive finding came from mutation, not from reading: re-introducing the original defect (ensure_real_engine_inputs put back into _real_engine_smoke_status) PASSED the first version of this guard. The reason is the whole point of the bug -- a check that writes its artifacts only when they are missing writes nothing on a machine that already has them, so on this tree (and on the machine of anyone who ever ran the old code) the write is invisible, and the wrong COUNT only ever appears on a machine that does not have them yet. The test now parks an existing build/ with a same-filesystem rename (instant despite gigabytes) so every run starts from the fresh-clone state, snapshots before and after each of two consecutive runs -- the second starting from the tree the first settled, which is where the count was corrupted -- and restores the parked tree in a finally, discarding what the run generated so the working tree ends exactly as it began (verified: build/ listing byte-identical). A second defect was found in my own test the same way: the two-run version asserted only changed and removed, so a creation outside the allowlist slipped through; the two cases are now one test that asserts created, changed and removed on both runs. | Proven load-bearing by mutation, each reverted and verified byte-identical afterwards: M1 the original defect -> run 1 fails naming build/real_engine_smoke/inputs/{real_engine_dialogue.txt,speaker_a_ref.wav,speaker_b_ref.wav} as created; M2 the snapshot made to return nothing -> the vacuity test fails (without it, every version of this guard passes for free); M3 'build' added to the snapshot's excluded directories -> the git-ignored-inclusion test fails; M4 a NEW check caching its result into Output/logs/doctor_cache.json -> run 1 fails naming it, which is the property this adds: a future check that writes is caught even though no existing check compares its output. scripts/doctor.py was restored after each run with `git diff --stat` verified empty. | Honest limits, stated plainly: files above 1 MiB are compared by size and mtime rather than content, so a rewrite that preserves both size and mtime in a multi-megabyte binary would not be seen (every source, plan, JSON and text file in the repo is hashed in full); the property covers the doctor process, not the GUI or the CLI, which is where the gate's checks live; and the snapshot window is a few seconds wide, so a concurrent writer in this shared checkout could report a change it did not cause -- the failure names every path, so that is diagnosable rather than mysterious. Suites: 957 passed / 0 failed / 0 errors (this tree, 193s, up from 169s for the ~24s the guard costs); 956 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed / 0 errors (clean archive copy, 199 tracked files, no .venv and no build/ at all, so the fresh-clone path -- the state the bug actually lived in -- is the one CI exercises). Nothing pushed.
